course: dictionary
lesson: 2817
title: Words for places
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A district is an area of a town, city or country. Often it has its own name, like the Lake District, or its own purpose, like a shopping district. «A girl in the Lake District went off alone to explore near camp» tells you the Lake District is a named area of countryside.
explain: Governments also mark out districts for jobs like running schools, voting and courts. «In many US school districts parents and school boards have argued about removing some library books» shows that a school district is an area whose schools are run together. The words school boards and parents are clues.
explain: Use the swap check to test your meaning. Put "area" in place of district and see if the sentence still works. «The Badger» says the animals have given the district a bad name, which means the local area around them. If the sentence still makes sense, your meaning is right.
fact: A district is an area of a town, city or country.
fact: A district often has its own name, like the Lake District, or its own purpose, like a shopping district.
fact: A school district is an area marked out by the government to run its local schools.
fact: Districts can also be used for voting and for courts.
fact: Lots of words can be checked with the swap check: put your meaning in the sentence and see if it still makes sense.
exercise: «A girl in the Lake District went off alone to explore near camp.» What is the Lake District? | A kind of lake you can swim in | A school for swimming | *A named area of countryside | A shop that sells boats
exercise: «In many US school districts parents and school boards have argued about removing some library books.» What does a school district do? | It is a shop that sells school books | It is a court for school trials | *It runs the local schools in an area | It is a team that looks after library books
exercise: Which phrase names a district with a special purpose? | fast car | tall tree | sunny day | *shopping district
test: «D.C. stands for District of Columbia, and the capital was put outside every state.» Why was it put outside every state? | Because it was the biggest state | So that it would be closer to the sea | So that it could have more sunshine | *So that no single state would control the national government
test: «The fog came down, and she got lost in the district.» In this sentence, what does district mean most closely? | A pair of walking shoes | *An area of countryside | A kind of fog | A type of rescue team
exam: Which of these is an area marked out by the government for voting, schools or courts? | A rescue team | A library | A park | *A district
